---

<Layout
  title={`Life Processes | CBSE Class 10 Science (Biology) | ${provider.name}`}
  description="Comprehensive revision notes, biochemical pathways, interactive SVG diagrams (Double Circulation & Nephron), and 20 CBSE Board MCQs for Life Processes: Nutrition, Respiration, Transportation, and Excretion."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Life Processes
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Science (Biology) · Chapter 5/6</span>
      <h1>Life Processes: Nutrition, Respiration, Circulation &amp; Excretion</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Master the four foundational pillars of cellular and organismal maintenance: enzymatic digestion, ATP generation pathways, human double circulation, and nephronic filtration with 20 board exam questions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>1. Autotrophic &amp; Heterotrophic Nutrition</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌱 Autotrophic Nutrition (Photosynthesis)</h3>
          <p class="formula">6CO₂ + 12H₂O + Sunlight + Chlorophyll &rarr; C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ + 6H₂O</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Three Crucial Events:</strong>
              <br/>1. <em>Absorption</em> of light energy by chlorophyll.
              <br/>2. <em>Conversion</em> of light energy into chemical energy and <em>photolysis</em> of water into hydrogen and oxygen.
              <br/>3. <em>Reduction</em> of carbon dioxide to carbohydrates (glucose stored as starch).
            </li>
            <li><strong>Desert Plant Adaptation:</strong> Desert plants (xerophytes) take up CO₂ at night and store it as an intermediate organic acid, which is acted upon by sunlight absorbed during the day.</li>
            <li><strong>Stomatal Mechanism:</strong> Guard cells swell when water flows into them, opening the stomatal pore; they shrink and close the pore when water leaves them.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🍽️ Heterotrophic Digestion (Human Alimentary Canal)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Mouth &rarr; Oesophagus &rarr; Stomach &rarr; Small Intestine &rarr; Large Intestine</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Mouth (Oral Cavity):</strong> Salivary amylase (ptyalin) converts starch into maltose. Tongue rolls food into a bolus; peristalsis moves it down the oesophagus.</li>
            <li><strong>Stomach:</strong> Gastric glands secrete:
              <br/>• <em>Hydrochloric Acid (HCl):</em> Creates an acidic medium (pH 1.5–2.5) to activate pepsin and kill microbes.
              <br/>• <em>Pepsin:</em> Protein-digesting enzyme (active only in acidic medium).
              <br/>• <em>Mucus:</em> Shields the inner stomach lining from excoriation by HCl.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Small Intestine (Site of Complete Digestion):</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Bile (Liver):</em> Neutralizes acidic chyme and emulsifies large fat globules into micro-droplets.
              <br/>• <em>Pancreatic Juice:</em> Trypsin (digests proteins) + Pancreatic Lipase (breaks down emulsified fats) + Amylase.
              <br/>• <em>Villi &amp; Microvilli:</em> Finger-like projections richly supplied with blood vessels and lacteals that maximize surface area for nutrient absorption.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Respiration & Transportation -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>2. Respiration &amp; Double Circulation</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚡ Breakdown of Glucose Pathways</h3>
          <p class="formula">Glycolysis (Cytoplasm): 1 Glucose (6C) &rarr; 2 Pyruvate (3C) + 2 ATP</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Pathway A (Absence of O₂ · Yeast Fermentation):</strong>
              <br/>Pyruvate &rarr; Ethanol (2C) + CO₂ + 2 ATP. Used in brewing and baking.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Pathway B (Lack of O₂ · Human Muscle Cells during Sprinting):</strong>
              <br/>Pyruvate &rarr; Lactic acid (3C) + 2 ATP. Accumulation of lactic acid causes muscle cramps.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Pathway C (Presence of O₂ · Mitochondria · Aerobic Respiration):</strong>
              <br/>Pyruvate &rarr; 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + 36–38 ATP. High-energy yield storing energy in ATP (the cellular energy currency).
            </li>
            <li><strong>Alveolar Exchange:</strong> Millions of balloon-like alveoli in lungs provide an expansive surface area (~80 m²) with ultra-thin walls surrounded by capillaries for gas diffusion.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>❤️ Human Cardiovascular System &amp; Plant Transport</h3>
          <p class="formula">Double Circulation: Pulmonary Circuit + Systemic Circuit</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Why Double Circulation?</strong> Humans are warm-blooded and require high energy to maintain constant body temperature; separating oxygenated and deoxygenated blood completely prevents mixing.</li>
            <li><strong>Heart Cycle:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Right Atrium &rarr; Right Ventricle:</em> Receives deoxygenated blood via Vena Cava and pumps it to lungs via Pulmonary Artery.
              <br/>• <em>Left Atrium &rarr; Left Ventricle:</em> Receives oxygenated blood from lungs via Pulmonary Vein and pumps it to the whole body through the thick-walled Aorta.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Blood Vessels:</strong> Arteries (thick elastic walls, high pressure, no valves); Veins (thin walls, low pressure, internal pocket valves preventing backflow).</li>
            <li><strong>Plant Transport Systems:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Xylem:</em> Unidirectional transport of water and dissolved minerals driven by root pressure and transpiration pull.
              <br/>• <em>Phloem:</em> Bidirectional translocation of sucrose and amino acids from leaves to sinks utilizing ATP energy.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Excretion Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>3. Human Excretory System &amp; Nephron Physiology</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🧪 Nephron: The Functional Filtration Unit</h3>
          <p class="formula">Ultrafiltration &rarr; Selective Tubular Reabsorption &rarr; Tubular Secretion</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Bowman's Capsule &amp; Glomerulus:</strong> Afferent arteriole brings blood under high pressure into the cup-shaped Bowman's capsule; initial filtrate contains glucose, amino acids, salts, urea, and excess water (~180 litres/day).</li>
            <li><strong>Tubular Reabsorption:</strong> As the filtrate moves along the convoluted tubules and Loop of Henle, essential nutrients (100% of glucose, amino acids) and 99% of water are reabsorbed back into the peritubular capillary network depending on the body's hydration state.</li>
            <li><strong>Final Urine:</strong> Concentrated urea, uric acid, excess salts, and water collect in the collecting duct (~1.5 to 2 litres/day), flowing through ureters to the urinary bladder.</li>
            <li><strong>Artificial Kidney (Hemodialysis):</strong> Uses semi-permeable cellophane tubes submerged in a dialyzing fluid with the same osmotic pressure as normal blood, except lacking nitrogenous wastes. No reabsorption occurs in hemodialysis.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌿 Excretion in Plants &amp; Comparative Biology</h3>
          <p class="formula">Gaseous Waste Diffusion · Waste Storage in Vacuoles &amp; Resins</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Oxygen:</strong> Regarded as a waste byproduct of daytime photosynthesis, released directly via stomata and lenticels.</li>
            <li><strong>Excess Water:</strong> Eliminated continuously via transpiration through stomatal pores.</li>
            <li><strong>Cellular Waste Storage:</strong> Many plant wastes are stored in cellular vacuoles, in leaves that periodically fall off, or stored as old resins and gums in non-functional xylem heartwood.</li>
            <li><strong>Root Excretion:</strong> Plants also excrete certain organic waste substances into the surrounding rhizosphere soil.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Cardiovascular Flow &amp; Nephronic Filtration</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <article class="card visual-card">
